# Computer power control through Sleep-On-LAN.
#
# Sleep-On-LAN (https://github.com/SR-G/sleep-on-lan, Apache-2.0) is a small
# service for Windows and Linux that runs a configured command when it gets an
# HTTP request. Installed on a computer (a vMix or presentation PC, a signage
# player), it lets OpenAVC shut the computer down, restart it, or put it to
# sleep or hibernation gracefully. Power On is a Wake-on-LAN magic packet sent
# from OpenAVC, so it works while the computer is off.
#
# Protocol, from the project's README and its source at tag 1.1.1-RELEASE
# (src/listener_http.go, src/sleeper_windows.go, src/utils_system.go):
#   - HTTP on port 8009 (the "HTTP:8009" listener). Every route is a GET.
#   - Basic auth on every route once the config file has an "Auth" block.
#   - GET /<operation> runs the command configured under that operation name
#     and answers at once, before the command finishes (it runs in a
#     goroutine). Reply with ?format=JSON: {"Operation":"<name>","Result":true}.
#     An operation that is not configured has no route: 404.
#   - "sleep" is configured by default (a SetSuspendState call on Windows).
#     Any other operation exists only when the computer's sol.json defines it.
#     This driver expects shutdown, restart and hibernate; the setup help says
#     what to put in sol.json.
#   - GET /state/local?format=JSON answers {"State":"online","Host":"localhost"}.
#   - GET /?format=JSON answers the agent's identity: Application, Version,
#     Commit, its network interfaces, listeners and configured commands.
#   - A configured command is split on spaces and run directly, with no shell,
#     so a path with spaces in it cannot be used (sol.json's own note).
#
# Push or poll: the agent has no notifications. It is polled, and the poll is
# also what notices the computer has gone off: the agent stops answering.
#
# Checked on real equipment (1.1.0): a Windows 11 PC running Sleep-On-LAN
# 1.1.1 as a service, with the sol.json from the setup help. Basic auth works
# and a wrong password answers 401; the reply field names above are the real
# ones; an operation sol.json does not define answers 404; Shut Down and
# Restart work; Power On wakes the PC from a full shutdown. The agent labels
# its JSON replies text/plain, which the json: responses below read anyway.
#
# Why YAML: plain HTTP GETs with JSON replies and Basic auth, plus a Wake-on-LAN
# datagram beside the connection, all fit ConfigurableDriver.

id: sleep_on_lan
name: Computer Power (Sleep-On-LAN)
manufacturer: Generic
category: utility
version: 1.1.0
min_platform_version: "0.36.0"
author: OpenAVC
description: >
  Turn a Windows or Linux computer on and off from OpenAVC: Wake-on-LAN to
  power it on, and shut down, restart, sleep or hibernate through the free
  Sleep-On-LAN service installed on the computer.

source_url: https://github.com/SR-G/sleep-on-lan
tags: [computer, pc, windows, power, shutdown, sleep, hibernate, wake-on-lan, wol]
verified: true
simulated: true
protocols: [sleep_on_lan_http]
ports: [8009]

transport: http

help:
  overview: >
    Turns a computer on and off. Power On sends a Wake-on-LAN packet, so it
    works while the computer is off. Shut Down, Restart, Sleep and Hibernate go
    to the Sleep-On-LAN service on the computer, which closes Windows properly
    instead of cutting the power. The device shows as offline while the
    computer is off or asleep.
  setup: >
    On the computer:
    1. Download Sleep-On-LAN 1.1.1 (github.com/SR-G/sleep-on-lan, Releases)
    and unzip it to a folder with no spaces in its path, such as C:\SleepOnLAN.
    2. Next to sol.exe, create sol.json with a login and the commands this
    driver uses:
    {"Listeners": ["HTTP:8009"], "Auth": {"Login": "openavc", "Password":
    "choose-one"}, "HTTPOutput": "JSON", "Commands": [
    {"Operation": "sleep", "Type": "internal-dll"},
    {"Operation": "shutdown", "Command": "C:\\Windows\\System32\\shutdown.exe /s /t 0 /f"},
    {"Operation": "restart", "Command": "C:\\Windows\\System32\\shutdown.exe /r /t 0 /f"},
    {"Operation": "hibernate", "Command": "C:\\Windows\\System32\\shutdown.exe /h"}]}
    3. Install it as a Windows service so it runs before anyone signs in (the
    project's README shows how, with NSSM), and allow TCP 8009 in Windows
    Firewall.
    4. For Power On: turn on Wake on LAN in the computer's BIOS, turn off
    Windows Fast Startup, and allow the network adapter to wake the computer
    with a magic packet. Use the wired network port.

    In OpenAVC: enter the computer's IP address, the login and password from
    sol.json, and the MAC address of its wired network adapter.

    Shut Down and Restart close every program without asking, so stop a
    recording or a stream first.

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 8009
  ssl: false
  verify_ssl: true
  auth_type: "basic"
  username: ""
  password: ""
  mac_address: ""
  timeout: 5.0
  poll_interval: 10

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
    description: "The computer's IP address. Give it a fixed address so OpenAVC can always find it."
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 8009
    label: Port
    description: "The port in sol.json's Listeners (HTTP:8009 unless you changed it)."
  auth_type:
    type: enum
    values: ["none", "basic"]
    default: "basic"
    label: Authentication
    description: "Basic when sol.json has an Auth login (recommended); none when it has no Auth block."
  username:
    type: string
    default: ""
    label: Login
    description: "The Login in sol.json's Auth block."
  password:
    type: string
    default: ""
    label: Password
    description: "The Password in sol.json's Auth block."
    secret: true
  mac_address:
    type: string
    default: ""
    label: MAC Address
    description: "The computer's wired network adapter, for Power On (Wake-on-LAN). Shown by 'ipconfig /all' as Physical Address."
  timeout:
    type: number
    default: 5.0
    label: Request Timeout (sec)
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 10
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    description: "How often OpenAVC checks that the computer is on."

state_variables:
  agent_state:
    type: string
    label: Computer State
    help: "online while the Sleep-On-LAN service answers."
  agent_version:
    type: string
    label: Sleep-On-LAN Version
  last_operation:
    type: string
    label: Last Command
    help: "The last power command the computer accepted (shutdown, restart, sleep, hibernate)."
  last_operation_ok:
    type: boolean
    label: Last Command Accepted

commands:
  power_on:
    label: Power On
    help: >
      Sends a Wake-on-LAN packet to the computer's MAC address. The computer
      starts up (or wakes from sleep or hibernation) if Wake on LAN is set up
      on it. Works while the computer is off.
    available_offline: true
    udp:
      magic_packet: mac_address

  shut_down:
    label: Shut Down
    help: "Shuts the computer down. Programs close without asking, so unsaved work, a recording or a stream stops."
    confirm: "Shut the computer down? Every program on it closes without saving, a recording or stream included."
    method: GET
    path: "/shutdown?format=JSON"

  restart:
    label: Restart
    help: "Restarts the computer. Programs close without asking."
    confirm: "Restart the computer? Every program on it closes without saving, a recording or stream included."
    method: GET
    path: "/restart?format=JSON"
    # Measured on a Windows 11 PC with an NVMe drive: silent 14 s after
    # Restart, answering again at 41 s. Restart time differs a lot from one
    # computer to the next (disk, startup programs, updates being installed),
    # so the window is generous; it ends the moment the computer answers.
    restarts_device_for: 300

  sleep:
    label: Sleep
    help: "Puts the computer to sleep. Programs stay open. Whether Power On wakes it from sleep depends on the computer: test it, and use Shut Down where it does not."
    confirm: "Put the computer to sleep? A recording or stream on it stops."
    method: GET
    path: "/sleep?format=JSON"

  hibernate:
    label: Hibernate
    help: "Hibernates the computer: it saves what is open and turns off. Power On wakes it with programs as they were."
    confirm: "Hibernate the computer? A recording or stream on it stops."
    method: GET
    path: "/hibernate?format=JSON"

  run_operation:
    label: Run Operation
    help: "Runs another command set up in sol.json, by its Operation name."
    method: GET
    path: "/{operation}?format=JSON"
    params:
      operation:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Operation
        help: "The Operation name from sol.json's Commands list."
        pattern: "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$"

  query_status:
    label: Query Status
    help: "Asks the Sleep-On-LAN service whether it is running."
    method: GET
    path: "/state/local?format=JSON"

  query_agent:
    label: Query Agent
    help: "Reads the Sleep-On-LAN version."
    method: GET
    path: "/?format=JSON"

quick_actions: [power_on, shut_down, sleep]

responses:
  # GET /state/local
  - json: true
    require: State
    set:
      agent_state: State

  # GET /<operation>: the agent accepted the operation (it runs afterwards).
  - json: true
    require: Operation
    set:
      last_operation: Operation
      last_operation_ok: Result

  # GET /: the agent's identity.
  - json: true
    require: Application
    set:
      agent_version: Version

on_connect:
  - query_agent
  - query_status

polling:
  queries:
    - query_status

simulator:
  initial_state:
    agent_state: online
    agent_version: "1.1.1-RELEASE"
    last_operation: ""
    last_operation_ok: false

  command_handlers:
    - match: '^GET /state/local(\?.*)?$'
      handler: |
        respond('{"State":"' + str(state["agent_state"]) + '","Host":"localhost"}')

    - match: '^GET /(shutdown|restart|sleep|hibernate)(\?.*)?$'
      handler: |
        op = match.group(1)
        state["last_operation"] = op
        state["last_operation_ok"] = True
        respond('{"Operation":"' + op + '","Result":true}')

    - match: '^GET /(\?.*)?$'
      handler: |
        respond('{"Application":"sleep-on-lan","Version":"' + str(state["agent_version"]) + '","Commit":""}')
